00:00:52,449 --> 00:00:54,983
this brand new VPS is fully up to date.

23
00:00:55,657 --> 00:00:58,002
And I'll do that still as my root user here.

24
00:00:58,549 --> 00:01:02,516
And as that root user on a Ubuntu system, I

25
00:01:02,645 --> 00:01:06,532
can use the apt command for managing my

26
00:01:06,580 --> 00:01:09,295
packages, my software on that server.

27
00:01:09,873 --> 00:01:13,150
That stands for Advanced Package Tool, and

28
00:01:13,150 --> 00:01:15,720
it's very common on Debian-based

29
00:01:15,720 --> 00:01:17,519
distributions like Ubuntu.

30
00:01:18,450 --> 00:01:22,065
On other distributions, you might have dnf,

31
00:01:22,466 --> 00:01:25,564
as a package manager with commands like dnf

32
00:01:25,788 --> 00:01:29,737
install or dnf upgrade for installing or

33
00:01:29,769 --> 00:01:32,048
upgrading packages. But in general, you

34
00:01:32,048 --> 00:01:34,087
should of course look up the documentation

35
