Ubuntu, as mentioned, it's apt.

38
00:01:41,486 --> 00:01:44,182
And there you can run apt install to install

39
00:01:44,391 --> 00:01:47,489
a new package or like in our case, upgrade

40
00:01:47,665 --> 00:01:50,490
to upgrade a package to install its latest

41
00:01:50,490 --> 00:01:50,795
version.

42
00:01:51,517 --> 00:01:53,843
And in order to run that, you first need to

43
00:01:53,843 --> 00:01:58,270
run apt update. So not upgrade, but update.

44
00:01:58,896 --> 00:02:02,569
And update will first update the registry of

45
00:02:02,681 --> 00:02:03,211
packages

46
00:02:03,788 --> 00:02:06,740
this server is aware of. So there is an

47
00:02:06,836 --> 00:02:08,745
official package registry

48
00:02:09,322 --> 00:02:11,809
which registers all the packages you could

49
00:02:11,809 --> 00:02:14,728
install with apt. And in order to make sure

50
00:02:14,728 --> 00:02:16,541
that we have the latest version of that

51
00:02:16,541 --> 00:02:20,836
registry on this new VPS here, we run apt

52
00:02:21,046 --> 00:02:22,674
update as a first step.

53
00:02:24,678 --> 00:02:26,799
So that gets us the latest information about

54
00:02:26,831 --> 00:02:29,275
which packages are available and what their

55
00:02:29,564 --> 00:02:30,641
latest versions are.

56
00:02:31,557 --> 00:02:33,891
And after running this, you can run apt

57
00:02:33,972 --> 00:02:37,767
upgrade to pull those latest versions that

58
00:02:37,865 --> 00:02:40,265
were now identified and install them. Now,

59
00:02:40,314 --> 00:02:42,390
that will not install all packages that are

60
00:02:42,390 --> 00:02:44,715
available, just the latest versions of the

61
00:02:44,781 --> 00:02:46,664
packages you already have on your system.

62
00:02:47,416 --> 00:02:50,621
If you add -y here, you will not be asked

63
00:02:50,621 --> 00:02:52,801
for confirmation and instead it will just

64
00:02:52,801 --> 00:02:54,980
download and install all these updates in

65
00:02:55,108 --> 00:02:57,752
one go without asking you for permission for

66
00:02:57,832 --> 00:02:58,489
every package.

67
00:02:59,451 --> 00:03:01,390
Now that can of course take a while until

68
00:03:01,518 --> 00:03:03,777
all these latest versions are installed,

69
00:03:04,563 --> 00:03:04,851
but then

70
00:03:05,604 --> 00:03:06,293
in the end here,

71
00:03:06,822 --> 00:03:07,896
it is done.

72
00:03:08,441 --> 00:03:09,963
Now here it's telling me that

73
00:03:10,492 --> 00:03:12,639
I should consider rebooting

74
00:03:13,296 --> 00:03:15,396
And I'll do that by running reboot, which is

75
00:03:15,396 --> 00:03:17,961
another handy command, of course. Now that

76
00:03:18,041 --> 00:03:20,894
terminates my connection naturally because

77
00:03:20,926 --> 00:03:23,203
that remote server is restarting.

78
00:03:24,116 --> 00:03:26,136
And therefore I'm back on my Mac here and I

79
00:03:26,200 --> 00:03:28,909
can reestablish my connection by rerunning

80
00:03:28,942 --> 00:03:30,496
that SSH command from before.

81
00:03:31,426 --> 00:03:33,013
And you can simply wait a couple of seconds

82
00:03:33,013 --> 00:03:35,113
and then try again and it should be up

83
00:03:35,113 --> 00:03:37,999
again. So with that, we made sure that the

84
00:03:37,999 --> 00:03:39,922
software installed on that server is up to

85
00:03:39,922 --> 00:03:41,974
date, which is always a good idea to patch

86
00:03:42,231 --> 00:03:44,010
any security holes that might have been

87
